    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:score-family",
      "def:normal-space",
      "def:quotient-gradient",
      "lem:normal-affine-identity",
      "thm:quotient-efficiency"
    ],
    "justification": "The factorization equation determines when choosing one representative can equal global across-pair efficiency.",
    "gap": "ZhangLiMiaoTchetgen2023Nonuniqueness establishes locally efficient regular inference under its additional regularity, while CuiEtAl2024SemiparametricProximal obtains single-pair efficiency under uniqueness and surjectivity; neither gives this two-fiber attainment criterion.",
    "consumer": "The SUPPORT workflow can test whether fitted bridge scores leave variance-reducing affine directions.",
    "proof_tex": "By \\(\\mathrm{lem:normal-affine-identity}\\), a shifted pair has score\n\\[\n\\phi_{h+u,q+m}=\\phi_0+\n\\sum_{a=0}^1t_a\\{F_a(h_a,m_a)-G_a(q_a,u_a)-R_a(m_a,u_a)\\}.\n\\]\nConditional on \\(\\mathrm{thm:quotient-efficiency}\\), the unique minimum-norm element of \\(\\mathcal A_P=\\phi_0+\\mathcal C_P\\) is\n\\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\phi_0-\\Pi_{\\mathcal C_P}\\phi_0\\).  Equality of the shifted score and this unique minimizer is therefore equivalent, after subtracting \\(\\phi_0\\), to\n\\[\n\\Pi_{\\mathcal C_P}\\phi_0\n=\\sum_{a=0}^1t_a\\{-F_a(h_a,m_a)+G_a(q_a,u_a)+R_a(m_a,u_a)\\},\n\\]\nwhich is the asserted factorization.  If it has no solution, every individual pair is distinct from the unique Hilbert projection and consequently has strictly larger squared norm.  Since the minimum belongs to the closed affine hull, finite affine combinations of elements of \\(\\Phi_P\\), expressible using the pure and four-corner identities, converge to it in \\(L^2(P)\\).",
